WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
YUFENG GUO: Google Cloud's
AI Platform Training now

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.500
has built-in
algorithms that you can

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.530
use to train models without
writing a single line of code.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:10.800
Let's dive in and find
out how you can use it.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.228
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.228 --> 00:00:14.608
[MECHANICAL SOUNDS]

00:00:16.036 --> 00:00:18.416
[SWOOSH]

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:21.890
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:25.950
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:26.990
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.260
And on this episode, we're
going to use AI Platform's

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.220
built-in algorithms
to train and deploy

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.320
a machine-learning model without
writing any training code.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.830
The tool acts as a handy wrapper
around AI Platform Training,

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:43.340
and it bridges the gap between
AutoML and full custom training

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:44.390
jobs.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:47.900
Now, as of this recording,
this feature is still in beta.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.450
But perhaps if you're
watching this in the future,

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:53.360
it may already be
generally available.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.760
Now, before we can
really get going on this,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.620
we want to decide
what it is we're

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.060
going to build a model for.

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.180
In this case, I've got
here the US Census data

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.060
set, a classic
binary classification

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.960
machine-learning problem.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.280
This means that the goal will be
to separate this data into one

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.780
of two groups.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.870
In this case, our data comes
from the 1994 US Census.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:18.140
And the task will be to
predict if a given household

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:22.670
income will be above or
below $50,000 a year.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.440
With this context
in place, let's look

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:28.460
at how we can use AI Platform
Training to train up this model

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.080
without writing any code.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.750
To get started with AI
Platform Training using

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.820
built-in algorithms,
head on over

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.340
to the Jobs menu in
the Cloud console

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.590
and click on New Training Job.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.930
In that menu, you
will see two options.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.730
Click on Built-in
Algorithm Training.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.510
The first step is to
select our algorithm.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.230
Currently, there are a
handful of options mostly

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:52.730
around structured data, though,
there are a few about images

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.100
as well which we
won't cover today.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.660
The three structured
data algorithms

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:00.140
that are currently available
are XGBoost, a Linear Learner,

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.600
and the Wide and Deep Learner.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.530
As the names suggest, XGBoost
uses the popular implementation

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:10.310
of gradient-boosted trees, while
the Linear Learner essentially

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:12.230
models something like
linear regression.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:14.180
And finally, the
Wide and Deep Learner

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.550
uses the TensorFlow
research model

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:20.060
Wide and Deep which tries
to combine the memorization

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:24.110
benefits of a wide linear model
with the strong generalization

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:26.810
capabilities of deep
neural networks.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.700
Once you've selected
an algorithm,

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.040
we need to specify
the training data.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.820
This system takes as input the
Google Cloud Storage URL path.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.480
So your data will need to be
uploaded onto Google Cloud

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:39.660
Storage beforehand.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.350
And additionally,
for structured data,

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:46.610
this system is expecting
specifically a headerless CSV

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:47.810
file.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.390
And furthermore, the
prediction target column

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:53.930
must be the very first
column of that CSV file.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:55.490
So it may mean
that you need to do

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:57.260
a bit of light
preprocessing just

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.010
to move things around a bit.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.980
And if your CSV
file is small enough

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:04.105
to load into a spreadsheet
software of your choice,

00:03:04.105 --> 00:03:05.480
I would recommend
just using that

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.810
and dragging your label column
over into that first column

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:09.770
position.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:12.650
Then just delete that
header row, if there is one,

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.065
and export the
file as a CSV file.

00:03:15.065 --> 00:03:16.940
This is probably the
most straightforward way

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.910
to generate that
appropriate output format.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:23.900
The system allows you to specify
a validation data and test

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:27.230
data, either as separate
files or as a percentage

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:28.710
of the training data.

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:32.120
So if your data is mixed in all
together, using the percentages

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.130
is an easy and convenient
way to ensure that you

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:36.440
have representative metrics.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.380
If you have separate files for
Training, Validation, and Test,

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.680
make sure that all
three are representative

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.660
of the same reality.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.460
Otherwise, your training
results, oh, they

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:48.420
might surprise you.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.900
So once you've specified all
the different training data,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.620
the next screen is all about
specifying the algorithm

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:56.750
arguments.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.930
This menu will differ depending
on which algorithm you choose.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:02.510
Each one has a somewhat
long list of parameters

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:03.970
that you can specify.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.970
Now, while many default
values are provided,

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:10.130
there is also extensive
documentation for each value

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:12.020
that you can set.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:14.090
Additionally, a lot
of these parameters

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:16.579
are available to be
used for HyperTune,

00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:20.690
which is Google Cloud Platform's
hyperparameter tuning service.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:23.540
To use it, check
the HyperTune box,

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.640
and provide a lower and upper
bound for the hyperparameter

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.310
that you want to have
the system tune for you.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.128
Be sure to check off Allow early
stopping of HyperTune trials.

00:04:33.128 --> 00:04:34.670
This will allow you
to take advantage

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:38.210
of early stopping of any
unpromising trials, which

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:39.950
will save compute and time.

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:42.560
Once your algorithm arguments
are set, click Next,

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.390
and we just have a
few final details

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:46.400
to provide about
the training job.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:50.160
Give it a job ID, region,
and select the scale tier,

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.190
and then the training
will commence.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:55.220
While the model is training,
you can make a coffee,

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.860
maybe read some web comics,
or kick off another training

00:04:57.860 --> 00:04:59.180
job in parallel.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.580
Since it's pretty lightweight
to start a training job,

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:04.580
you can try different models
and different parameter ranges

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:05.750
pretty easily.

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:07.490
Once training is
complete, you can

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:09.750
click on the job to view
some of the details,

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.230
including resource
utilization as well as links

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:13.850
to Stackdriver Logging.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:18.020
You can also deploy your model
directly from that screen.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.610
There's a Deploy Model
button at the top, which

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:22.650
will grab the exported
model from Google Cloud

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:25.140
Storage for you and
deploy it out so you

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.810
can have a REST API to call
your models for predictions.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:30.450
So there you have
it, an easy way

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:32.460
to run model training
and deployment

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.560
without needing to write
a single line of code

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.390
while still having
the controls that you

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:40.450
want for adjusting the
details of a particular model.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:42.600
This can save time
while still enabling

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:46.740
you to get high-quality results
customized for your use case

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:48.240
and data set.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.110
For more details and
examples, check out the links

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:53.380
included in the
description below.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.100
Thanks for watching this episode
of "Cloud AI Adventures."

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.050
And if you enjoyed it,
click that Like button,

